  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Prove that the difference of the squares of two odd integers is always divisible by 8.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(2a+1)^2-(2b+1)^2=4[a(a+1)-b(b+1)]$, which is divisible by $8$.\n\nBoth consecutive-integer products are even.",
      "worked": "Write the odd integers as $2a+1$ and $2b+1$, where $a,b\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(2a+1)^2-(2b+1)^2&=(4a^2+4a+1)-(4b^2+4b+1)\\\\(2a+1)^2-(2b+1)^2&=4a^2+4a-4b^2-4b\\\\(2a+1)^2-(2b+1)^2&=4[a(a+1)-b(b+1)].\\end{align*}$$\nEach product of consecutive integers is even. Thus $a(a+1)=2r$ and $b(b+1)=2s$ for integers $r,s$, giving\n$$(2a+1)^2-(2b+1)^2=8(r-s).$$\nTherefore the difference is divisible by $8$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
